    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Build the Service Bus job handler that completes the inbound capture pipeline: it dequeues the job that task 030's Event Grid ingress enqueued, normalizes the ACS chat event, and persists it idempotently.

    • **ACS-event normalizer** — map the ACS `ChatMessageReceivedInThread` (and sibling) event payload to a channel-agnostic `NormalizedMessage` (the envelope the engine/enrichment operate over). This is the ACS analog of `GraphMessageNormalizer` — the mapping happens exactly once, at the pipeline boundary, so nothing downstream sees `Azure.Communication.*`.
    • **Idempotent persist** — hand the `NormalizedMessage` to task 021's `ICommunicationChannelIngestor` (`MessagingIngestor`) to persist `sprk_communication` (type=Message) and invoke enrichment. The persist MUST be **idempotent**: dedupe on the **ACS message id** via the existing `IIdempotencyService` (`TryAcquireProcessingLockAsync` / `IsEventProcessedAsync` / `MarkEventAsProcessedAsync`). This single dedupe covers three duplicate sources: Event Grid at-least-once redelivery, Event Grid genuine duplicates, and **our own outbound echo** — ACS emits a `ChatMessageReceivedInThread` for a message we sent persist-on-send (task 051), and that echo must be a no-op because the outbound message id is already recorded. (The `sprk_thread` lookup is set later by task 040's `IThreadResolver`; this task persists the record — thread assignment is a downstream concern.)
    • **Dead-letter from day one** — on repeated processing failure (after the contract's retry/backoff), dead-letter the job to Storage; do NOT silently drop. Enrichment/thread side-effects are best-effort and MUST NOT fail the persist (NFR-02).

    The invariant to hold: **exactly one persisted `sprk_communication` per distinct ACS message id, regardless of how many times the event is delivered or whether it is our own echo.**
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Spaarke BFF Communication architect. Authority on `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Communication/` inbound + `Services/Jobs/` (idempotency, DLQ, retry). Fluent in ADR-045 (channel seams — ingestor), ADR-046 (ACS transport — task 007), ADR-004/ADR-036 (job contract / idempotency / DLQ), ADR-010 (DI), ADR-032 (Null-Object), ADR-038 (integration-heavy tests), and `.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md`. Reasons carefully about at-least-once delivery, duplicate suppression, and echo-dedup race conditions.
  </role>

  <goal>
    A Service Bus job handler dequeues the ACS-event job (from 030), normalizes the event to a `NormalizedMessage` (ACS analog of `GraphMessageNormalizer`), and persists via `MessagingIngestor` idempotently — deduping on the ACS message id through `IIdempotencyService` so at-least-once redelivery, genuine duplicates, and our own outbound echo each yield **exactly one** persisted `sprk_communication`. Repeated failures dead-letter to Storage; enrichment/thread side-effects are best-effort/non-fatal (NFR-02). Build clean; idempotency + echo-dedup + DLQ tests pass; publish-size (vs ~45.30 MB) + CVE + bff-extensions Placement Justification reported; `/conflict-check` before PR.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      **Mirror the email inbound job; add ACS-specific idempotency.** The email inbound path runs a Service Bus job (`IncomingCommunicationJobHandler` → `IncomingCommunicationProcessor`) over the `Services/Jobs/` contract with `IIdempotencyService` + DLQ. Messaging's job is the ACS analog: dequeue → normalize ACS event → `MessagingIngestor` persist → enrichment (design §4 inbound + §8.3). Task 030 built the Event Grid ingress that enqueues the job; task 021 shipped the `ICommunicationChannelIngestor` seam + `MessagingIngestor`; `NormalizedMessage` already exists as the channel-agnostic envelope. This task writes the ACS normalizer + the idempotent job handler that ties them together.

      **Why xhigh / correctness-critical (design §8.3, §4 echo-dedup):** Event Grid is **at-least-once, unordered, and may duplicate**. Additionally, outbound is **persist-on-send** (ADR-045 rule 3 / FR-04, task 051): when we send a message, we persist it directly AND ACS emits a `ChatMessageReceivedInThread` echo of our own message. Without dedupe, that echo double-persists. The seam that reconciles persist-on-send (outbound) with capture-on-event (inbound) WITHOUT collision is a single idempotency check on the ACS message id — the same key covers redelivery, duplicates, and echo. Getting the lock/mark ordering wrong (e.g. mark-before-persist on a crash, or a race between two concurrent deliveries of the same id) either double-persists or drops the message. Reason about the acquire-lock → check-processed → persist → mark-processed ordering carefully; a concurrent duplicate must lose the race cleanly.

      **§11 Component Justification.** The ACS normalizer is NEW: (Existing) `GraphMessageNormalizer` maps Graph→envelope but cannot read ACS event schema; no ACS normalizer exists (grep); (Extension) it is a sibling normalizer at the same boundary, not a modification of the Graph one (per-channel mapping is the ADR-045 pattern); (Cost-of-doing-nothing) without it, ACS events cannot become a `NormalizedMessage`, so the ingestor/engine/enrichment can't process inbound chat — capture is impossible. The job handler is NEW: without it, jobs 030 enqueues are never processed.

      Scope discipline: normalizer + idempotent job handler + DLQ wiring ONLY. Do NOT set the `sprk_thread` lookup (040's `IThreadResolver`), build the outbound send path (051 — this task only guarantees the echo is a no-op via dedupe), or modify the Event Grid ingress (030). Reuse `IIdempotencyService` + the existing DLQ (`DeadLetterQueueService`) — do NOT hand-roll a dedupe store or a second DLQ.
    </background>
